A||||4||ChadMan||So who do you trust for 88 steering parts?||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||08-23-2001||03:08 PM||||Have any of you 88 owners replaced your outer tie rod ends...and if so, where'd you get them?<P>I've decided to break down and replace my inner and outer tie rod ends along with the rack bushing.  I've already got the rack bushing from Rodney, and I will order the inner tie rod ends from him too.<P>But I'm a little concerned about the outers.  Fiero Store has them, but they're $70/each - ouch!  Advance says they don't have 'em.  Autozone claims to have them for $30/side, but they have sold me 84-87 parts before out of ignorance, as has NAPA, and I really don't want my car out of commission for a week while I wait for Fiero Store parts because the local yokels sold me the wrong thing.<P>Any suggestions?||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||08-23-2001||03:44 PM||||I bought my outers from Checker Auto......they were TRW brand, but I believe they are all the same (Moog, TRW, etc), just repackaged, like the aftermarket slave cylinders.  I paid $40 apiece for them (with tax), so the $30 from AZ is reasonable.  The part # should be 3238......there may be other letters in there, but 3238 should be in it (Moog # is ES3238RL, for example).  If you have doubts, have the clerk look up the outer tie rod for an '87 Fiero and compare the part numbers.....if they're different then you know you'll get the right one.<P>I replaced everything I could on the front end of my daily driver '88 GT......new inner & outer tie rods, new rack bushing (original GM!), new control arm bushings, new upper & lower balljoints, etc.  After the alignment and new tires I can honestly say what a difference it makes!  My 113K mile GT rides better than my 16K mile GT!<P>The only thing I did not like about the outer tie rods was the dust boot and how it is attached.  The originals have a nice "tall" area for the boot to slide onto......the boot also has a snapring that keeps it in one place.  The aftermarket one has a "shorter" area for the dust boot and it is just plain rubber (no snapring).....the compression of being attached to the spindle is all that keeps it in place.  Other than that, no problems.<P>The only way I'd pay the $70 for the Fiero Stores tie rods is if they are original GM......then it might be worth the extra $$.<P>Good luck!<P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||08-24-2001||09:37 AM||||Thanks, mrfiero...you've always got the good info.  Now I feel informed enough to try Autozone first and save some $$$.<P>Autozone did say they'd have to order the outers from a warehouse in TN.  That's a good sign as I doubt they'd have 88 parts lying around every store.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||08-30-2001||09:12 AM||||Just want to thank you again.  Went to Autozone...sure enough, their part number was ??3238.  They had just one in stock, so I bought that one and had them order the other.<P>I've got inners on the way from Rodney.  A week from Saturday, I'll be putting new tie rod ends on the Coupe.  I can't wait!<P>How tight do you suppose my front end will be with:<P>New inner tie rod ends<BR>New outer tie rod ends<BR>New steering rack bushing<BR>3-year-old lower ball joints<BR>Original upper ball joints<BR>Original suspension bushings||||reg||6
Z||000004||mrfiero||08-30-2001||11:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>Just want to thank you again.  Went to Autozone...sure enough, their part number was ??3238.  They had just one in stock, so I bought that one and had them order the other.<P>I've got inners on the way from Rodney.  A week from Saturday, I'll be putting new tie rod ends on the Coupe.  I can't wait!</B><P><BR>I'm glad I could help.<P><B><BR>How tight do you suppose my front end will be with:<P>New inner tie rod ends<BR>New outer tie rod ends<BR>New steering rack bushing<BR>3-year-old lower ball joints<BR>Original upper ball joints<BR>Original suspension bushings</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>It will be tighter than it was, but I wouold strongly suggest replacing the lower balljoints while you have everything apart.  They take the most abuse and you might have balljoint issues in the near future (may as well take care of it all at once).<P>The upper balljoints don't take as much abuse, so you should still be OK there.  However.....I would also suggest replacing the bushings if you can.  Inspect them and if they look like they have deteriorated then replace them.  The lowers are a piece of cake, but the uppers are a pain in the butt.  Of course, the uppers are the most likely to need replacing as they wear out faster than the lowers.<P>At any rate, even if you don't replace the balljoints or bushings your Fiero will drive MUCH better than it did.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
